SOL.update({
21:[("Introduce the slope",r"The quantity \(\frac{y}{x}\) is the slope of the line from the origin to \((x,y)\). Let \(t=\frac{y}{x}\), so \(y=tx\)."),("Substitute into the ellipse",r"Substituting \(y=tx\) gives \[(2+t+3t^2)x^2-(11+20t)x+40=0.\] For a given \(t\), this line intersects the ellipse when this quadratic has a real solution."),("Use the discriminant",r"The extreme values of \(t\) occur when the line is tangent, so the discriminant is \(0\): \[(11+20t)^2-160(2+t+3t^2)=0.\]"),("Simplify",r"This becomes \[-80t^2+280t-199=0,\] or \[80t^2-280t+199=0.\]"),("Use sum of roots",r"The maximum and minimum slopes are the two roots of this quadratic. Their sum is \(\frac{280}{80}=\frac72\)."),("Conclude",r"The answer is $\boxed{\frac72}$."),],
22:[("Use the magic product",r"Let the common product be \(P\). The diagonal \(50,e,2\) gives \(P=100e\)."),("Use the other diagonal",r"The other diagonal gives \(ceg=100e\), so \[cg=100.\]"),("Relate entries using rows and columns",r"From the first row, \(50bc=100e\), so \(bc=2e\). From the middle column, \(beh=100e\), so \(bh=100\). From the bottom row, \(2gh=100e\), so \(gh=50e\)."),("Derive g in terms of b",r"Using \(gh=50e\) and \(bh=100\), divide the first by the second to get \(\frac{g}{b}=\frac{e}{2}\). Also \(bc=2e\) and \(cg=100\) imply \(\frac{g}{b}=\frac{50}{e}\). Hence \(e=10\) and \(g=5b\)."),("Apply integrality",r"Now \(c=100/g=20/b\), \(d=2e/g=4/b\), and \(h=100/b\). Thus \(b\) must divide \(4\), so \(b=1,2,4\). The corresponding \(g\)-values are \(5,10,20\)."),("Conclude",r"Their sum is \(5+10+20=\boxed{35}\)."),],
23:[("Name the roots",r"Let the integer root be \(r\), and let the other two positive roots be \(u\) and \(v\). The problem says \(r=u+v\)."),("Use the sum of roots",r"By Vieta's formula, \(r+u+v=2004\). Since \(r=u+v\), this gives \(2r=2004\), so \(r=1002\)."),("Reduce to the other two roots",r"The other two roots have sum \(1002\). Since the polynomial has integer coefficients and \(r\) is an integer root, the quadratic factor has integer coefficients, so \(q=uv\) must be an integer."),("Find the possible range",r"The roots \(u\) and \(v\) are positive and distinct, so \[0<q<\left(\frac{1002}{2}\right)^2=251001.\] Thus \(q=1,2,\ldots,251000\)."),("Exclude integer roots",r"The roots \(u,v\) would be integers exactly when the discriminant \(1002^2-4q\) is a perfect square. Since it is divisible by \(4\), write it as \(4k^2\). Then \(q=251001-k^2\), with \(k=1,2,\ldots,500\), giving \(500\) excluded values."),("Count n values",r"Each valid \(q\) gives \(n=-1002q\), so the number of possible \(n\)-values is \[251000-500=250500.\] The answer is \(\boxed{250,500}\)."),],
24:[("Set angle variables",r"Let \(\alpha=\angle DBE\) and \(\beta=\angle DBC\). Then \(\angle CBE=\alpha-\beta\), and because \(AB=BC\), \(\angle ABE=\alpha+\beta\)."),("Use the geometric progression",r"The tangent values form a geometric progression, so \[\tan(\alpha-\beta)\tan(\alpha+\beta)=\tan^2\alpha.\] Let \(u=\tan\alpha\) and \(v=\tan\beta\). Using tangent formulas, this simplifies to \(u^4=1\), so \(u=1\)."),("Use the arithmetic progression",r"The cotangent values form an arithmetic progression: \[2\cot(\alpha-\beta)=\cot\alpha+\cot\beta.\] Since \(\tan\alpha=1\), this becomes \[2\cdot\frac{1+v}{1-v}=1+\frac1v,\] so \(v=\frac13\)."),("Translate to lengths",r"In right triangle \(BDE\), \(\tan\alpha=\frac{DE}{BD}=1\), so \(DE=BD\). Since \(BE=10\), this is a \(45^\circ-45^\circ-90^\circ\) triangle, and \(BD=5\sqrt2\)."),("Find the base and area",r"Also \(\tan\beta=\frac{DC}{BD}=\frac13\), so \(DC=\frac{BD}{3}\). Because \(AB=BC\), \(D\) is the midpoint of \(AC\), so \(AC=2DC=\frac{2BD}{3}\)."),("Conclude",r"The area is \[\frac12\cdot AC\cdot BD=\frac12\cdot\frac{2BD}{3}\cdot BD=\frac{BD^2}{3}=\frac{50}{3}.\] The answer is $\boxed{\frac{50}{3}}$."),],
25:[("Use digit-length blocks",r"Since \(2^{2004}\) is a \(604\)-digit number beginning with \(1\), the powers \(2^0,2^1,\ldots,2^{2003}\) cover digit lengths \(1\) through \(603\). For each digit length, the first power of \(2\) with that length begins with \(1\). Thus \(603\) elements begin with \(1\)."),("Double leading digits",r"Doubling a number beginning with \(1\) produces a number beginning with \(2\) or \(3\). Doubling again produces a number beginning with \(4,5,6,\) or \(7\)."),("Count three large groups",r"Therefore \(603\) elements begin with \(1\), \(603\) begin with \(2\) or \(3\), and \(603\) begin with \(4,5,6,\) or \(7\)."),("Find the remaining group",r"The set has \(2004\) elements, so the number beginning with \(8\) or \(9\) is \[2004-3\cdot603=195.\]"),("Relate 4 to 8 or 9",r"A power of \(2\) begins with \(4\) exactly when the next power begins with \(8\) or \(9\). This pairing stays inside the given range."),("Conclude",r"Thus \(195\) elements begin with \(4\), so the answer is \(\boxed{195}\)."),],
})
